---- packet framing ----
ok    a small payload
ok    sequence id is carried
ok    an empty payload
ok    a maximum-size payload becomes two packets
ok      the first is full and marked so
ok      and the second is empty, terminating it
ok    an ordinary payload is one packet
---- length-encoded integers ----
ok    encoded
ok    decoded
ok    0xFB decodes as the NULL marker
ok    a length-encoded string
ok    an EMPTY string is not null
ok      and really is not null
ok    the NULL marker is null
---- handshake ----
ok    parsed
ok    server version
ok    connection id
ok    auth plugin
ok    nonce length
ok    nonce is both halves in order
ok    a truncated handshake is refused, not fatal
ok    protocol 9 is refused
ok    PROTOCOL_41 is requested
ok    CONNECT_WITH_DB is requested when there is one
ok    PLUGIN_AUTH is requested
ok    DEPRECATE_EOF is requested
ok    MULTI_STATEMENTS is NOT requested
ok    no CONNECT_WITH_DB without a database
ok    the response names the user
ok    and the database
ok    and the plugin
ok    auth switch plugin
ok    auth switch nonce keeps 20 bytes
---- authentication ----
ok    mysql_native_password
ok    caching_sha2_password fast path
ok    native response is 20 bytes
ok    sha2 response is 32 bytes
ok    an empty password sends nothing
ok      for sha2 too
ok    native is supported
ok    sha2 is supported
ok    an unnamed plugin defaults to native
ok    an unknown plugin is refused
ok    mysql_clear_password is refused in the clear
ok    and answered over TLS
ok    an unknown plugin is still refused over TLS
ok    clearPassword is NUL-terminated
---- OK and ERR packets ----
ok    affected rows
ok    last insert id
ok    in a transaction
ok    a 16-bit affected count
ok    a 64-bit insert id
ok    error number
ok    sqlstate
ok    mapped class
ok    composed message
---- errno to DBERR_* ----
ok    errno 1062
ok    errno 1451
ok    errno 1452
ok    errno 1048
ok    errno 1213
ok    errno 1205
ok    errno 1040
ok    errno 1064
ok    errno 1146
ok    errno 1054
ok    errno 1045
ok    errno 1049
ok    errno 2013
ok    an error with no sqlstate still maps
ok      and keeps its message
---- column definitions and rows ----
ok    column name
ok    column type
ok    a utf8 varstring is not binary
ok    a binary-charset blob is binary
ok    a utf8 blob is TEXT, not bytes
ok    the unsigned flag is read
ok    text row field count
ok      a value
ok      a NULL
ok      an empty string, which is NOT null
ok      and is empty
ok    binary row field count
ok      column 0
ok      column 1 is NULL, from bit 3
ok      column 2 follows the gap
ok    a row with no nulls
ok      the string came through
---- prepared statements ----
ok    parsed
ok    statement id
ok    column count
ok    parameter count
ok    COM_STMT_PREPARE
ok    COM_QUERY
ok    COM_STMT_CLOSE
ok    COM_QUIT
ok    COM_STMT_EXECUTE
ok    COM_STMT_EXECUTE with no parameters
---- values ----
-- text protocol --
ok    int
ok    bigint at i64 min
ok    double
ok    text
ok    NULL
ok    empty string
ok    decimal is text, not a float
ok    json is text
-- binary protocol --
ok    int4
ok    int4 negative
ok    the same bytes, unsigned
ok    int8
ok    double is exact
ok    double 1/3 is exact
ok    float4
ok    blob keeps its bytes
ok    date
ok    datetime
ok    datetime with microseconds
ok    the zero date
ok    time
ok    time beyond a day
ok    a negative interval
-- parameters --
ok    int becomes LONGLONG
ok    float becomes DOUBLE, as bits
ok    text is length-encoded
ok    a blob keeps every byte
ok    NULL has no data
ok    NULL is typed as MT_NULL
ok    a hostile value goes verbatim, unescaped
---- a whole session, replayed ----
ok    connected
ok    no error
ok    server version was absorbed
ok    connection id
ok    the query succeeded
ok    row count
ok    column names
ok    row 0
ok    row 1 name is empty but NOT null
ok    row 1 name
ok    row 2 name IS null
ok    the handshake response named the user
ok    COM_STMT_PREPARE was sent
ok    COM_STMT_EXECUTE was sent
---- an INSERT, where MySQL beats Postgres ----
ok    the insert succeeded
ok    no result columns
ok    affected rows
ok    lastInsertId is real here
ok      and on the connection too
ok    changes()
---- an error, replayed ----
ok    the query reports failure
ok    classified as a constraint violation
ok    the server's wording survives
ok    the raw errno is reachable
ok    and the sqlstate
---- authentication failures, replayed ----
ok    not open
ok    classified as an open failure
ok    the server's message is passed through
ok    the connection fails rather than hanging
ok    and explains that it needs TLS or RSA
ok    and names the fix
ok    and warns that it is intermittent
ok    mysql_clear_password is refused
ok      because it would send the password in the clear
---- a packet sequence that does not line up ----
ok    the connection is refused
ok    and says the stream is out of step
---- USE invalidates every cached statement ----
ok    the first query worked
ok      three rows
ok    USE succeeded
ok    the same SQL was re-prepared after the USE
ok      and still returns its rows
---- the same session, one byte at a time ----
ok    connected over a dribbling transport
ok    the query succeeded
ok    same row count
ok    same first row
ok    row 2 is still null
---- the server hangs up mid-session ----
ok    the handshake completed
ok    the query fails rather than hanging or panicking
ok    classified as closed
---- TLS negotiation ----
ok    an unknown ssl-mode does not connect
ok    and is classified as unsupported
ok    verify-full is accepted as an alias for VERIFY_IDENTITY
ok    a DSN with no ssl-mode connects
ok    and sent the full handshake response, not a 32-byte SSLRequest
ok    PREFERRED connects in the clear when TLS is not offered
ok    REQUIRED does not fall back
ok    and says the server does not offer TLS
ok    a transport with no socket cannot be upgraded
ok    and says exactly that
ok    only the SSLRequest went out
ok    header is 32 bytes at sequence 1
ok    CLIENT_SSL is set
ok    the negotiated capabilities came with it
ok    max packet
ok    charset
ok    23 reserved zeros
ok    buffered plaintext blocks the upgrade
ok    and says what was left over
---- the dialect ----
ok    name
ok    identifiers are backquoted
ok    no RETURNING
ok    no IF NOT EXISTS on CREATE INDEX
ok    introspection is now supported
ok    tables SQL maps TABLE_TYPE onto SQLite's words
ok    tables SQL is scoped to one database
ok    columns SQL makes cid 0-based
ok    columns SQL takes the pk ordinal from SEQ_IN_INDEX
ok    columns SQL uses MySQL's placeholder
ok    indexes SQL emits SQLite's origin words
ok    index-column SQL reads direction from COLLATION
ok    foreign-key SQL groups multi-column constraints
ok    objects SQL emits the literal 'trigger'
-- tables --
SELECT t.TABLE_SCHEMA AS `schema`, t.TABLE_NAME AS `name`, CASE t.TABLE_TYPE WHEN 'BASE TABLE' THEN 'table' WHEN 'VIEW' THEN 'view' WHEN 'SYSTEM VIEW' THEN 'view' ELSE 'unknown' END AS `kind`, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM information_schema.COLUMNS c WHERE c.TABLE_SCHEMA = t.TABLE_SCHEMA AND c.TABLE_NAME = t.TABLE_NAME) AS ncol, 0 AS withoutrowid, 0 AS strict FROM information_schema.TABLES t WHERE t.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() ORDER BY t.TABLE_NAME
-- columns --
SELECT (c.ORDINAL_POSITION - 1) AS cid, c.COLUMN_NAME AS `name`, c.COLUMN_TYPE AS decltype, CASE WHEN c.IS_NULLABLE = 'NO' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS notnull, c.COLUMN_DEFAULT AS dflt, COALESCE(s.SEQ_IN_INDEX, 0) AS pkpos, CASE WHEN c.EXTRA LIKE '%STORED GENERATED%' THEN 3 WHEN c.EXTRA LIKE '%VIRTUAL GENERATED%' THEN 2 ELSE 0 END AS hidden FROM information_schema.COLUMNS c LEFT JOIN information_schema.STATISTICS s ON s.TABLE_SCHEMA = c.TABLE_SCHEMA AND s.TABLE_NAME = c.TABLE_NAME AND s.COLUMN_NAME = c.COLUMN_NAME AND s.INDEX_NAME = 'PRIMARY' WHERE c.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND c.TABLE_NAME = ? ORDER BY c.ORDINAL_POSITION
-- indexes --
SELECT (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY s.INDEX_NAME) - 1) AS seq, s.INDEX_NAME AS `name`, CASE WHEN MIN(s.NON_UNIQUE) = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS `unique`, CASE WHEN s.INDEX_NAME = 'PRIMARY' THEN 'pk' WHEN MIN(s.NON_UNIQUE) = 0 THEN 'u' ELSE 'c' END AS origin, 0 AS `partial` FROM information_schema.STATISTICS s WHERE s.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND s.TABLE_NAME = ? GROUP BY s.INDEX_NAME ORDER BY s.INDEX_NAME
-- index columns --
SELECT (s.SEQ_IN_INDEX - 1) AS seqno, COALESCE(c.ORDINAL_POSITION - 1, -1) AS cid, COALESCE(s.COLUMN_NAME, '') AS `name`, CASE WHEN s.COLLATION = 'D' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS `desc`, '' AS coll, 1 AS `key` FROM information_schema.STATISTICS s LEFT JOIN information_schema.COLUMNS c ON c.TABLE_SCHEMA = s.TABLE_SCHEMA AND c.TABLE_NAME = s.TABLE_NAME AND c.COLUMN_NAME = s.COLUMN_NAME WHERE s.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND s.TABLE_NAME = ? AND s.INDEX_NAME = ? ORDER BY s.SEQ_IN_INDEX
-- foreign keys --
SELECT (DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY k.CONSTRAINT_NAME) - 1) AS id, (k.ORDINAL_POSITION - 1) AS seq, k.REFERENCED_TABLE_NAME AS reftable, k.COLUMN_NAME AS fromcol, COALESCE(k.REFERENCED_COLUMN_NAME, '') AS tocol, r.UPDATE_RULE AS onupdate, r.DELETE_RULE AS ondelete FROM information_schema.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE k JOIN information_schema.REFERENTIAL_CONSTRAINTS r ON r.CONSTRAINT_SCHEMA = k.CONSTRAINT_SCHEMA AND r.CONSTRAINT_NAME = k.CONSTRAINT_NAME WHERE k.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND k.TABLE_NAME = ? AND k.REFERENCED_TABLE_NAME IS NOT NULL ORDER BY k.CONSTRAINT_NAME, k.ORDINAL_POSITION
-- objects --
SELECT 'table' AS `type`, t.TABLE_NAME AS `name`, t.TABLE_NAME AS tblname, '' AS sql FROM information_schema.TABLES t WHERE t.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND t.TABLE_TYPE = 'BASE TABLE' UNION ALL SELECT 'view', v.TABLE_NAME, v.TABLE_NAME, v.VIEW_DEFINITION FROM information_schema.VIEWS v WHERE v.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() UNION ALL SELECT 'index', s.INDEX_NAME, s.TABLE_NAME, '' FROM information_schema.STATISTICS s WHERE s.TABLE_SCHEMA = DATABASE() AND s.SEQ_IN_INDEX = 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'trigger', g.TRIGGER_NAME, g.EVENT_OBJECT_TABLE, g.ACTION_STATEMENT FROM information_schema.TRIGGERS g WHERE g.TRIGGER_SCHEMA = DATABASE() ORDER BY 1, 2
---- done ----
